# Files that are executed on the Proxmox host must keep LF endings.
#
# CRLF in a shell script is not cosmetic: bash makes the \r part of the token,
# so `set -u` becomes `set -u\r` ("invalid option"), `do` becomes `do\r`
# ("syntax error near unexpected token"), and paths gain a trailing \r. A
# driver script deployed with CRLF on 2026-08-24 died instantly on every
# launch while reporting a valid PID.
#
# Python and YAML tolerate CRLF, which is why this only ever bites the shell
# scripts — and why it is easy to miss when the same edit "works" for .py.
# The same reasoning reaches further than the Proxmox host. An .ipxe script is
# parsed by iPXE out of a UEFI environment during network boot, where there is
# no shell to be forgiving and no way to read an error that scrolls past before
# the host resets. It is published to the depot verbatim.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.ipxe  text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts run on Windows but 17 of the repo's 18 .ps1 files were
# already LF, and the one CRLF outlier produced a 515-line diff for a 19-line
# change on 2026-09-30 -- churn that buries real edits in a safety-critical
# review. PowerShell reads LF fine on Windows, so pin the majority convention
# rather than let editors flip files back and forth.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.psm1  text eol=lf
*.psd1  text eol=lf
